    <h2>When the Door Won't Open, It's Usually Not the Opener</h2>

    <p>The opener gets blamed because it's the part with the motor and the wall button — the part that looks like it should be doing the work. It isn't. On a properly balanced door, the springs carry the weight and the opener does little more than nudge a counterbalanced door up and down. A standard door weighs well over a hundred pounds, and no consumer opener is built to hoist that dead weight on its own. So when the motor runs and the door stays put, the counterbalance is gone — almost always a broken torsion spring. Diagnosing that correctly is the difference between a couple-hundred-dollar spring and an unnecessary opener you didn't need.</p>

    <h2>Why a New Opener Is the Wrong First Fix</h2>

    <p>Here's the mistake that costs Shoals homeowners real money: a door quits, a big-box opener goes in over the weekend, and it seems to work — for a while. Then it fails early, because that opener has been fighting a door the dead spring left completely unbalanced, straining every cycle to lift weight it was never rated to carry. The gears strip, the motor cooks, and now you've bought an opener and still need the spring. The honest version of this trade tells a homeowner up front: a $500 opener will not fix a $250 spring problem, it'll just add a second bill on top of it.</p>

    <h2>The Spring Is the Part Living Under Load</h2>

    <p>Torsion springs are rated in cycles — a standard spring is often good for about 10,000 open-close cycles, which for a busy family door is roughly seven to ten years. They wear out on a schedule, and they usually go with a loud bang, sometimes when nobody's even near the garage. A practitioner's move on a spring call is to weigh the door's balance by hand with the opener disconnected, size the replacement spring to that weight, and replace both springs together even if only one broke — because the second is the same age and days from failing too. That balance test is the step a rushed crew skips, and it's why some "repaired" doors are back to wearing out openers within a year.</p>

    <h2>Old Single-Car Doors and New Double Doors Are Different Jobs</h2>

    <p>The Shoals housing split shows up in the garages too. The older detached garages behind Florence and Sheffield homes often carry original single-car doors on aging tracks, sometimes wood, sometimes needing the whole system modernized rather than patched — and honestly, a few of those old doors on bent tracks aren't worth repairing twice. The newer Muscle Shoals subdivisions run wide double and oversized doors that weigh more, need heavier springs, and fail differently. A contractor working the whole region has to stock and price for both a narrow 7-foot original and a 16-foot double, because they aren't the same repair.</p>

    <h2 id="pricing-summary">What Garage Door Work Costs in the Shoals</h2>

    <p>Real ranges, and what moves them:</p>
    <ul class="trades-list-block">
      <li><strong>Service call / diagnostic</strong> — about $75 to $150, often credited toward the repair.</li>
      <li><strong>Torsion spring replacement</strong> — roughly $200 to $450 installed, both springs at once.</li>
      <li><strong>New opener installed</strong> — around $400 to $700.</li>
      <li><strong>Single door replacement</strong> — about $1,000 to $2,500.</li>
      <li><strong>Double or oversized door replacement</strong> — $1,800 to $4,500, the newer-subdivision job.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>The spread on replacement is mostly door size and insulation — a bigger, better-built door on a Muscle Shoals double garage costs more than a basic single on an older Sheffield detached garage.</p>

    <h2>The Broken-Spring Emergency, and What Not to Do</h2>

    <p>A snapped spring is a same-day call — a car trapped inside a garage is an emergency, and forcing the door with a jammed opener risks bending panels and tracks that turn a $300 fix into a replacement. Do not try to hand-lift a door with a broken spring; that dead weight is exactly how people get hurt. And if the door has started reversing on its own or closing crooked, that's a balance or safety-sensor issue worth fixing before it becomes a pinch hazard, not something to keep operating around.</p>

